<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo River is the second longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> to the Atlantic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> occupied the lands south of its lower course. The river's mouth was reached by the Portuguese in the 1480s, leading to the Kongo Empire's contact with Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-river.jpg" alt="Congo River" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MONUSCO/Myriam Asmani · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Congo_River_near_Kisangani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Henry Morton Stanley completed his descent of the <b>Congo River</b> in 1877, Leopold hired him, and from 1879 to 1884 Stanley built stations along the river, including Vivi and Léopoldville, and gathered hundreds of treaties by which Congolese chiefs ceded land and rights to Leopold’s organisations, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their trade reached the Teke Kingdom and the <b>Congo River</b> beyond, and by the late eighteenth century slaves from the Bobangi area were a significant share of exports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Kongo emerged in the late fourteenth century in the region south of the lower <b>Congo River</b>, consolidating a series of Bantu-speaking communities into a centralised polity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1880 Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza signed a treaty with the Makoko, ruler of the Kingdom of Tio, placing land north of the <b>Congo River</b> under French protection; Brazzaville grew from his post.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a></li><li><blockquote>Kakongo was one of the Kikongo-speaking kingdoms north of the <b>Congo</b> estuary that recognised the senior Kingdom of Loango in early times.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakongo">Kingdom of Kakongo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ngoyo was one of three Kikongo-speaking kingdoms on the coast north of the <b>Congo</b> estuary, alongside the Kingdom of Loango and the Kingdom of Kakongo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ngoyo">Kingdom of Ngoyo</a></li></ul></section>
